WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.700
Reporting turns container review
observations into action.

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:09.949
A useful finding should identify the
affected image, component, manifest,

3
00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:14.229
pipeline, or runtime setting; provide
evidence references;

4
00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:18.105
explain risk in context; describe severity
reasoning;

5
00:00:18.185 --> 00:00:21.093
and give practical remediation guidance.

6
00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:25.212
The goal is not to overwhelm developers
with scanner noise.

7
00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:28.603
The goal is to help teams reduce
meaningful risk.

8
00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:31.187
Evidence references are important.

9
00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:36.436
A report may cite image digests,
Dockerfile lines, scanner report entries,

10
00:00:36.516 --> 00:00:40.474
SBOM components, registry metadata,
manifest snippets,

11
00:00:40.554 --> 00:00:44.188
runtime configuration exports, or policy
findings.

12
00:00:44.268 --> 00:00:49.842
Those references let others reproduce the
review and understand exactly what was

13
00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:50.729
evaluated.

14
00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:54.444
If evidence is based on a tag, a
point-in-time export,

15
00:00:54.524 --> 00:00:59.128
or a non-production environment, the
limitation should be stated.

16
00:00:59.208 --> 00:01:04.296
Remediation should be developer-friendly
and operationally realistic.

17
00:01:04.376 --> 00:01:08.253
It may involve updating base images,
rebuilding images,

18
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.937
removing unnecessary packages, improving
build secret handling,

19
00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:17.621
setting non-root users, reducing
capabilities, changing mounts,

20
00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:21.578
adding network policies, improving
registry controls,

21
00:01:21.658 --> 00:01:25.292
or creating backlog tickets for dependency
updates.

22
00:01:25.372 --> 00:01:28.199
Severity should reflect risk, exposure,

23
00:01:28.279 --> 00:01:31.672
and feasibility rather than scanner score
alone.

24
00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:35.952
AI can help draft clear language, organize
recommendations,

25
00:01:36.032 --> 00:01:38.535
and create ticket-ready summaries.

26
00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:41.200
Those drafts still need human review.

27
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.722
AI-generated remediation may be
incomplete, outdated, unsafe,

28
00:01:45.802 --> 00:01:48.145
or mismatched to the environment.

29
00:01:48.225 --> 00:01:52.102
A strong report preserves assumptions and
limitations,

30
00:01:52.182 --> 00:01:55.251
ties recommendations to verified evidence,

31
00:01:55.331 --> 00:02:00.500
and gives teams a practical path toward
safer containerized applications.
